root.Text:
Unable to muster the courage to commit to the jump, {name} gives up on the idea. {He/She}
 feels ashamed for {his/her} lack of courage and ends up hating jumping in general
 because it reminds {him/her} of {his/her} failure.

The warrior, as it turns out, is not hostile, but is understandably concerned about
 being spied on. After a fairly lengthy conversation, they reach an understanding. "Our people," the warrior 
 explained, "went through a devastating tragedy long ago. We lost our home and our loved ones
 and made our way to this place to escape our attackers. Ever since then, we lived in fear, waiting
 for the day our past would catch up to us. When I saw you strangers find us, I thought this was the end... But it seems you are kind,
 little one. Perhaps we were wrong about you." Little {name} shares an old story about how {his/her} 
 ancestors came to Isola after losing their home in a volcanic explosion, and recounts their tales of hardship and survival. The warrior's eyes soften and start to tear up.
{He/She} talks to {name}'s parents, who offer food items in thanks for looking after their child. At dinner, little {name} recalls what {he/she}
 has learned about the wants and needs of the strange, masked people, which surprises and intrigues the villagers. Perhaps this will come in handy later.

One day, an Isolan envoy comes to the villagers under a banner of peace. {He/She}
 claims to be interested in their ways and asks for a tour of their camp. {name} seems
 sincere, but some villagers whisper that {he/she} could be a spy.

{name} decides that the bowl must certainly be a hat and promptly puts it on. {He/She}
 likes how it shades {his/her} eyes from the sun. However, {he/she} also notices that
 the Isolans greatly admire the hat. It seems that skillful crafting is highly appreciated in their culture. {name}
 realizes that {he/she} has something in common with the Isolans, and is able to develop better relationships with them.

{name} feels nothing at first, but the next time {he/she} sees a child playing, {name}
 begins to develop a very strong parental instinct. {He/She} knows that it is {his/her}
 calling in life to raise children - lots and LOTS of children!
